# SIR for compare-versions@3.6.0 default export (the bare `compareVersions(v1, v2)` function).
# Comments use '#' only. This is a language-neutral IR, not JavaScript.
# Every behavioral claim below was VERIFIED by RUNNING the real package (see PROVENANCE / VERIFIED-BY-EXECUTION).

UNIT compareVersions
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG compareVersions(v1: string, v2: string) -> -1 | 0 | 1
DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime deps; no embedded data tables; pure string/number logic

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RE-EMITTER NOTE (default export, value-mode)
# The real package does `module.exports = compareVersions` (a bare function with .validate/.compare
# attached as static methods; ONLY the call form `compareVersions(v1, v2)` is in contract here).
# The blind re-emitter must `export default <fn>` where <fn> is exactly this two-argument function.
# The generator sets exportName = 'compareVersions', importKind = 'default'.
# The static methods compareVersions.validate / compareVersions.compare are OUT-OF-CONTRACT for this
# unit (see SCOPE) — do NOT implement them to satisfy this oracle; only the call form is graded.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BEHAVIOR
# Pipeline order is EXACT. Reproduce node-by-node.

# ===== NODE 1 — VALIDATION (runs on BOTH args, v1 then v2, BEFORE any comparison) =====
# Both arguments are validated, in the order [v1, v2]. Validation of v1 happens first; if v1 is
# invalid the function throws before v2 is examined.
#
# 1a. TYPE CHECK: if typeof(arg) !== 'string'  -> THROW TypeError
#       message EXACTLY: "Invalid argument expected string"
#       (applies to number/null/undefined/object/boolean/symbol/array — anything not a primitive string)
#
# 1b. SEMVER REGEX CHECK: if the string does NOT match the regex below -> THROW Error (NOT TypeError)
#       message EXACTLY (single-quote the offending string, interpolated verbatim):
#         "Invalid argument not valid semver ('" + version + "' received)"
#       e.g. for input "foo" the message is:  Invalid argument not valid semver ('foo' received)
#
#     THE EXACT REGEX (case-insensitive, ASCII; this is the literal source regex):
#       /^v?(?:\d+)(\.(?:[x*]|\d+)(\.(?:[x*]|\d+)(\.(?:[x*]|\d+))?(?:-[\da-z\-]+(?:\.[\da-z\-]+)*)?(?:\+[\da-z\-]+(?:\.[\da-z\-]+)*)?)?)?$/i
#
#     REGEX SEMANTICS, decoded (the blind engineer MUST honor these exactly — verified by execution):
#       - Optional single leading 'v' or 'V'  (the leading "v?", case-insensitive). Only ONE 'v' is
#         optional here; a second 'v' is NOT stripped/allowed: "vv1.0.0" is INVALID.
#       - MAJOR segment: one-or-more ASCII digits  (\d+). REQUIRED. Wildcards 'x'/'*' are NOT allowed
#         in the MAJOR position. So "x", "*", "x.0.0", "*.0.0" are INVALID. (A bare "x" or "*" THROWS.)
#       - MINOR segment (optional): a literal '.' then EITHER a single 'x'/'X'/'*' wildcard char OR \d+.
#       - PATCH segment (optional, only if MINOR present): '.' then a single 'x'/'X'/'*' OR \d+.
#       - FOURTH numeric segment (optional, only if PATCH present): '.' then 'x'/'X'/'*' OR \d+.
#         At MOST FOUR dot-separated numeric/wildcard segments total. "1.2.3.4" is valid;
#         "1.2.3.4.5" is INVALID.
#       - A wildcard is exactly ONE of the characters x, X, or * occupying the WHOLE segment.
#         "1.xx", "1.x0", "1.0x" are INVALID (wildcard must be a lone single char in its segment).
#       - PRE-RELEASE (optional): "-" followed by one-or-more identifiers separated by '.', each
#         identifier matching [\da-z\-]+ (ASCII digits, lowercase OR uppercase letters because of /i,
#         and the hyphen). PRE-RELEASE is ONLY allowed when PATCH is present (it is nested inside the
#         patch group). So "1.2.3-alpha" and "1.2.3.4-alpha" are valid, but "1-a" and "1.2-a" are
#         INVALID. A trailing empty identifier is invalid: "1.2.3-" and "1.0.0-a." are INVALID.
#         A lone hyphen identifier "1.0.0--" IS valid (hyphen is in the char class).
#       - BUILD METADATA (optional): "+" followed by identifiers [\da-z\-]+(\.[\da-z\-]+)* . Same
#         nesting: only allowed when PATCH is present. "1.2.3+b" valid; "1+b","1.2+b" INVALID;
#         "1.0.0+" INVALID (empty). Build may appear with or without pre-release; pre-release then
#         build is valid ("1.2.3-a+b").
#       - Anchored ^...$ : NO leading/trailing whitespace allowed (" 1.0.0" and "1.0.0 " INVALID).
#       - Leading zeros ARE allowed by \d+  ("01.2.3", "1.02.0" are VALID).
#       - The character class is ASCII-only (\d and a-z under /i = ASCII letters). Non-ASCII letters
#         and any other punctuation (",", "!", "/", space, etc.) make the string INVALID.
#
#     The static method compareVersions.validate(version) is exactly:
#         typeof version === 'string' && regex.test(version)
#     (returns boolean; does not throw). It is OUT-OF-CONTRACT for this unit but documents the same
#     predicate used by NODE 1b. Mentioned only to keep the regex semantics anchored.

# ===== NODE 2 — SPLIT each validated version into segments =====
# Define split(v) producing a string[] (the LAST element is always the pre-release tail, possibly ""):
#   c = v with a single leading 'v' removed (regex /^v/, lowercase v only — note: validation already
#       accepted 'V' too, but the strip regex /^v/ is lowercase-only and NOT case-insensitive;
#       in practice an uppercase 'V' prefix is left in place. Such inputs are NOT emitted by the
#       generator — see SELF-CONSISTENCY — so this corner is documented, not graded.)
#   c = c with build metadata removed: delete everything from the first '+' to end (regex /\+.*$/).
#   patchIndex = index of first '-' in c, or c.length if no '-'.
#   numericPart = c.substring(0, patchIndex)            # the dotted numeric/wildcard segments
#   preRelease  = c.substring(patchIndex + 1)           # everything after the first '-', or "" if none.
#                                                        # (If no '-', substring(len+1) = "".)
#   arr = numericPart.split('.')                        # e.g. "1.2.3" -> ["1","2","3"]
#   arr.push(preRelease)                                # append the pre-release tail as the LAST element
#   return arr
#
# CONSEQUENCE — array shapes (verified):
#   "1.2.3"            -> ["1","2","3",""]
#   "1.2"              -> ["1","2",""]
#   "1"                -> ["1",""]
#   "v1.2.3"           -> ["1","2","3",""]
#   "1.0.0-alpha"      -> ["1","0","0","alpha"]
#   "1.0.0+build"      -> ["1","0","0",""]           # build stripped, no pre-release
#   "1.0.0-alpha.1+m"  -> ["1","0","0","alpha.1"]    # build stripped from the tail
#   "1.x.0"            -> ["1","x","0",""]
#   "1.2.3.4"          -> ["1","2","3","4",""]
#   "1.0.0-"           -> ["1","0","0",""]           # NOTE "1.0.0-" is actually INVALID at NODE 1b, never reaches here
# The numeric segments are arr[0 .. length-2]; the pre-release string is arr[length-1].

# ===== NODE 3 — NUMERIC SEGMENT COMPARISON =====
# Let s1 = split(v1), s2 = split(v2).
# Loop i from 0 to (max(s1.length - 1, s2.length - 1) - 1)  inclusive  # i.e. over every numeric slot,
#   excluding the last (pre-release) element of each. The bound uses length-1 for each because the last
#   element is the pre-release tail, NOT a numeric segment.
#     n1 = parseInt(s1[i] || 0, 10)      # if s1[i] is missing (undefined) use 0 -> parseInt(0,10)=0;
#     n2 = parseInt(s2[i] || 0, 10)      #   if s1[i] is the empty string "" it is falsy -> 0 too.
#     if n1 > n2  return 1
#     if n2 > n1  return -1
#     # else (n1 === n2, including NaN===... see WILDCARDS) continue to next i
#
# parseInt semantics that MATTER (verified):
#   - parseInt is base-10 and reads the leading integer prefix. Leading zeros are ignored:
#     parseInt("01",10) === 1, so "01.0.0" == "1.0.0" (compares EQUAL).
#   - WILDCARDS: a wildcard segment is the literal "x", "X", or "*". parseInt("x",10) and
#     parseInt("*",10) are NaN. The comparisons (n1 > n2) and (n2 > n1) are BOTH false when either
#     operand is NaN, so a wildcard segment neither-greater-nor-less => treated as EQUAL to whatever
#     it is compared against AT THAT POSITION. Verified:
#       "1.x.5" vs "1.9.5" -> 0   (minor x compares equal, patch 5==5)
#       "1.x.5" vs "1.9.6" -> -1  (minor x equal, patch 5 < 6)
#       "2.x"   vs "1.0"   -> 1   (major 2 > 1, decided before the wildcard)
#       "1.x"   vs "2.0"   -> -1
#       "1.x.0" vs "1.5.0" -> 0
#   - UNEQUAL LENGTHS: missing numeric slots are treated as 0 (via `|| 0`). Verified:
#       "1.2" vs "1.2.0" -> 0   ("1.2" lacks the patch slot; treated as 0 == 0)
#       "1"   vs "1.0.0" -> 0
#       "1.2.3.4" exposes a 4th numeric slot vs a 3-segment version which is 0 there:
#       "1.0.0.5" vs "1.0.0" -> 1 ;  "1.0.0" vs "1.0.0.5" -> -1
#
# NOTE on loop bound + pre-release lengths: the bound is max over (length-1) of EACH array. Because
# each array's last element is the pre-release tail, length-1 = number of numeric segments. When one
# version has more numeric segments than the other, the extra slots on the short side read undefined
# -> `|| 0` -> 0.

# ===== NODE 4 — PRE-RELEASE COMPARISON (only reached when all numeric segments tied) =====
# sp1 = s1[s1.length - 1]   # the pre-release string (or "")
# sp2 = s2[s2.length - 1]
#
# CASE A — BOTH non-empty (sp1 && sp2 both truthy):
#   p1 = sp1.split('.').map(tryParse)
#   p2 = sp2.split('.').map(tryParse)
#   where tryParse(token) = isNaN(Number(token)) ? token (keep as string) : Number(token) (coerce to number)
#     IMPORTANT tryParse uses Number(), NOT parseInt. So Number-coercible tokens become NUMBERS:
#       Number("1")  = 1   -> number
#       Number("10") = 10  -> number
#       Number("0x10")=16  -> number   (hex literal coercion! a valid identifier under the regex)
#       Number("1e2")=100  -> number   (scientific coercion!)
#       Number("")   = 0   -> number   (empty string coerces to 0; but "" can't be a pre-release id under the regex)
#       Number("-")  = NaN -> stays STRING "-"
#       Number("alpha") = NaN -> stays STRING "alpha"
#     (These Number-coercion quirks differ from semver-spec's "all-digits" rule, but this is the
#      package's actual behavior — reproduce it. Tokens with leading zeros like "00" -> Number=0
#      number; an identifier that is all digits is numeric.)
#
#   Loop i from 0 to (max(p1.length, p2.length) - 1) inclusive. At each i, in THIS EXACT order:
#     (1) if p1[i] === undefined  OR (typeof p2[i] === 'string' AND typeof p1[i] === 'number')  return -1
#     (2) if p2[i] === undefined  OR (typeof p1[i] === 'string' AND typeof p2[i] === 'number')  return 1
#     (3) if p1[i] > p2[i]  return 1
#     (4) if p2[i] > p1[i]  return -1
#     # else continue
#   RULES decoded (verified):
#     - SHORTER pre-release loses: if p1 has fewer identifiers, p1[i] becomes undefined first -> rule(1)
#       -> -1. Verified: "1.0.0-1" vs "1.0.0-1.0" -> -1 ; "1.0.0-alpha" vs "1.0.0-alpha.1" -> -1.
#     - NUMERIC ranks BELOW ALPHANUMERIC at a given identifier slot: number-vs-string -> the numeric
#       side is lower. rule(1): p2 string & p1 number -> -1 ; rule(2): p1 string & p2 number -> 1.
#       Verified: "1.0.0-1" vs "1.0.0-alpha" -> -1 ; "1.0.0-alpha" vs "1.0.0-1" -> 1.
#     - NUMBER vs NUMBER: compared numerically by `>` (rule 3/4). "1.0.0-2" vs "1.0.0-10" -> -1.
#     - STRING vs STRING: compared by JS `>` (lexicographic by UTF-16 code unit, CASE-SENSITIVE).
#       Verified: "1.0.0-alpha" < "1.0.0-beta" -> -1 ; "1.0.0-beta" < "1.0.0-rc" -> -1 ;
#       "1.0.0-a" < "1.0.0-b" -> -1 ; "1.0.0-ab" > "1.0.0-aa" -> 1 ;
#       UPPERCASE sorts BEFORE lowercase ('A'=0x41 < 'a'=0x61): "1.0.0-Alpha" vs "1.0.0-alpha" -> -1.
#     - EQUAL: all identifiers equal & same length -> fall through to NODE 5 -> 0.
#       "1.0.0-alpha" vs "1.0.0-alpha" -> 0.
#
# CASE B — EXACTLY ONE non-empty (sp1 XOR sp2):
#   return sp1 ? -1 : 1
#   i.e. a version WITH a pre-release ranks BELOW the same version WITHOUT one. Verified:
#     "1.0.0-alpha" vs "1.0.0" -> -1 ;  "1.0.0" vs "1.0.0-alpha" -> 1.
#   (This branch is only reached when the numeric segments are all equal; if numeric segments differ
#    they already decided in NODE 3 — e.g. "1.0.0-alpha" vs "1.0.1" -> -1 by numeric, NOT by pre-release.)
#
# CASE C — BOTH empty: fall through to NODE 5.

# ===== NODE 5 — DEFAULT =====
# return 0   (versions are equal).

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Each oracle vector: args = [v1, v2]; expected = the real package's return (-1|0|1) OR, for invalid
# inputs, the harness-recorded throw sentinel {__throw: <message>}. The harness stamps expecteds by
# RUNNING the real package on genInputs; the re-emitter is graded structurally (a throw matches any
# throw per verdict.vectorPass; a numeric return must equal exactly).

SCOPE
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): all of NODE 1..5 above — validation (both throws + exact
#   messages + the literal regex), the split into numeric segments + pre-release tail, base-10
#   parseInt numeric comparison with `|| 0` for missing slots and NaN-as-equal wildcard semantics,
#   the four-rule pre-release comparison with Number()-based tryParse coercion (including hex/sci
#   coercion), pre-release-below-release ranking, shorter-pre-release-loses, numeric-below-alpha,
#   case-sensitive lexical string compare, and the default 0.
# CARRIED DATA: none. No constant tables; the only literal is the inline regex, which is reproduced
#   verbatim in NODE 1b above (it is small enough to transcribe and is part of the in-contract logic,
#   not a carried table).
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT (not graded by this unit's oracle; do NOT implement to satisfy it):
#   - compareVersions.validate(version): boolean predicate (documented above for anchoring only).
#   - compareVersions.compare(v1, v2, operator): operator-based boolean wrapper with its own
#     validateOperator throws ("Invalid operator type, expected string but got <typeof>" /
#     "Invalid operator, expected one of >|>=|=|<|<=") and operatorResMap. Separate unit; not here.
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (codec cannot round-trip; behavior documented, NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   - Non-string first/second args (number, null, undefined, object, array, boolean, symbol, function)
#     all THROW TypeError "Invalid argument expected string" at NODE 1a. A blind engineer reconstructs
#     this from NODE 1a. The generator emits ONLY string args (both valid and invalid strings), so the
#     graded inputs stay codec-faithful; the TypeError path is documented for fidelity but the throw
#     path that IS exercised by the generator is the Error path (NODE 1b) via invalid strings.
#   - Uppercase 'V' leading prefix: validation accepts it (regex /i) but split's /^v/ does not strip
#     it, leaving "V1.0.0" -> split major token "V1" -> parseInt("V1",10)=NaN. This corner is
#     documented but NOT emitted by genInputs (generator uses only lowercase 'v' prefix), to keep the
#     teaching/grading slices unambiguous.

# ===== SELF-CONSISTENCY CROSS-CHECK =====
# Every value class genInputs can emit is defined above:
#   - valid release versions (1..4 numeric segments, leading zeros, lowercase 'v' prefix) -> NODE 3.
#   - wildcard minor/patch/4th ('x','X','*') -> NODE 3 NaN-equal semantics.
#   - pre-release versions (alpha/beta/rc, numeric ids, mixed numeric.alpha, multi-id, hex/sci tokens
#     like 0x10/1e2, uppercase ids, lone-hyphen id) -> NODE 4 CASE A.
#   - one-side-pre-release pairs -> NODE 4 CASE B.
#   - build metadata (+...) which is stripped -> NODE 2.
#   - invalid strings (empty, "foo", too many segments, wildcard-in-major, pre-release without patch,
#     trailing '-', non-ASCII/punctuation, leading/trailing space, "*", "x") -> NODE 1b Error throw.
# The generator NEVER emits non-string args or uppercase-'V'-prefix (the two OUT-OF-ENVELOPE corners),
# so the SIR fully defines every input the oracle will stamp.

